## 2. The computation

Ljungström's `brunerie'` (Cubical/Homotopy/Group/Pi4S3/BrunerieExperiments.agda, both commits):

    brunerie' = g10 (g9 (g8 λ i j → f7' λ k → η₃ .fst (push (loop i) (loop j) k)))

with `g8 = mapΩrefl encodeTruncS²`, `g9 = encodeTruncS¹`, `g10 = SetTrunc.rec isSetℤ (idfun ℤ)`,
`f7' = f7stuff.encode north`. A two-dimensional loop in the join of two circles is pushed through
the Hopf-type encoding into the 4-truncation of the 2-sphere, decoded through the 3-truncation of
the circle to an integer: the Brunerie number, −2. Every step of that is a path computation: a
transport along a line of types given by univalence (`ua`), compositions in higher inductive types
(the truncations, the join, the spheres), a Glue type unglued. There is no numerical shortcut; the
answer comes out only because every one of the cubical rules computes.

Our port, experiments/brunerie/typed.cub: `brunerie = g10 (encodeTruncS1 g8)` with
`g8 = λ i → encodeTruncS2 (λ j → F i j)`, the same composition with `g9` and the λs written in; its
`K2` is the 4-truncation of `S2` as a higher inductive type with the eight-argument constructor
`squash4`, `CodeK` is the family over the suspension of `S2` given by `ua (K2eq a)`, `HopfS2` the Hopf
fibration as a Glue type. It is 82 pieces of text; with lib/core.cub it is 262 checked declarations,
all accepted. The checker that accepts them is lib/check.cub, a program of the language that the same
net runs; the result is read at the declared type `Int` and prints `negsuc (suc zero)`, which is −2.

What "the computation" measures on each side. In Agda: the check of the declaration
`test : brunerie' ≡ -2; test = refl`, which Agda's profiler accounts under Typing.CheckRHS: the two
sides are normalised and compared. On our side: the reading `#show brunerie` at type `Int`, which
builds the net for the term and runs it to its normal form: 395,608 interaction steps as the machine
now stands (358,691 with the free-dimension bound on, 405,568 with the whole cut off). Both include
building the term from its definition and nothing of loading or checking the definitions.

