## 4. How each evaluates, from the code

### 4.1 Agda

Terms are trees. `Agda/Syntax/Internal.hs` lines 346 onward: a `Term` is a variable with
eliminations, a λ, a literal, a defined name applied to eliminations, a constructor applied, a Π, a
sort, a level, a metavariable. There is no sharing node: two occurrences of a subterm are two trees.

Substitution is applied through the term. `Agda/TypeChecking/Substitute.hs` lines 904 to 950,
`applySubstTerm`: a substitution meeting a variable looks it up; meeting a λ, a definition applied, a
constructor applied, a Π, it rebuilds the node with the substitution pushed into every part; the
comment at 2026-03-17 says the intent is to create no thunks "until we hit the next inductive layer
of terms", so a substitution is eager one layer at a time, and the Π case computes sorts
(`mkPiSort`, which the profile below shows). Substituting a dimension variable by an interval
expression is this same operation: the whole term is rebuilt.

Two reducers. `Agda/TypeChecking/Reduce.hs` line 604: reducing a term tries the fast reducer for a
definition or constructor applied (`maybeFastReduceTerm`, lines 610 to 618), the slow one otherwise
(`slowReduceTerm`, 635 onward, which unfolds a definition by its compiled clauses and reduces path
applications by `reduceIApply`). The fast reducer, `Agda/TypeChecking/Reduce/Fast.hs`, describes
itself (lines 4 to 25): "a call-by-need environment machine with an implicit heap maintained using
STRefs", with memoised definition lookup and compacted case trees; closures are a term, an environment
and a spine of eliminations whose arguments are pointers to thunks (lines 493 to 620). So Agda does
share work inside the fast machine: an argument is a thunk evaluated once. But the machine handles
only what it knows (lines 100 to 120: functions, constructors, `primForce`, `primErase`, type
constructors, axioms, and primitives it can run on literals, `CPrimOp`; everything else is `COther`,
"in this case we fall back to slow reduction"). The primitives of the interval and the Kan operations
(`primTransp`, `primHComp`, `primComp`, Glue) are not in its list of literal primitives (lines 166 to
270 name natural-number, character and string primitives), so as far as I read they are `COther`. On
meeting one, `fallbackEval` (lines 1466 to 1478) "decode[s] the current focus to a Term, call[s] slow
reduction and pack[s] up the result in a value closure": the shared closures are written out as a
tree, reduced as a tree by the slow reducer, and the result re-entered as a value. A path application
whose dimension is 0 or 1 is taken by the machine itself (`evalIApplyAM`, lines 1439 to 1450);
one whose dimension is anything else is left in the spine.

The Kan operations. `Agda/TypeChecking/Primitive/Cubical.hs` line 414, `primTransHComp`: reduce the
face formula φ, view it; if it is 1, answer at once; otherwise reduce the type family, and go by cases
on what it is (Π, Σ, Path, a data type, Glue, the universe …), each case building the answer as a
term with `runNamesT`, a term-building monad over names. The face formula is decomposed where a
system is matched: `Agda/TypeChecking/Primitive/Cubical/Base.hs` lines 354 to 391,
`decomposeInterval'`: a conjunction is the cartesian product of its parts' assignments, a disjunction
their union, a negation flips them; the result is a list of partial assignments of the variables to
0 or 1 with leftover neutral terms. This is computed where it is needed, from the formula's syntax,
each time.

Conversion. `Agda/TypeChecking/Conversion.hs` line 144, `compareTerm`, through `compareAs` (152) and
`compareTerm'` (279): the two sides are reduced to weak head normal form and compared, constructor
against constructor, then their arguments, recursively; for `refl : brunerie' ≡ -2` the left side
is normalised until it is a constructor of ℤ and compared with `-2`. The profiler's counters on that
check (agda29-profile-compute.txt) are small: 15 comparisons, 5 by reduction; the work is inside the
one normalisation.

All of it runs in the type-checking monad, a state monad over the signature (every definition's
clauses, type, compiled form), the builtins table, the metavariables and the options; a reduction
step that needs a definition asks the monad for it (`getConstInfo`; the fast reducer memoises this,
line 8 to 11 of its header, because "a big chunk of the time during reduction is spent looking up
definitions in the signature").

### 4.2 The machine

Read in full this week: src/net.c (4366 lines), src/lang.c (5114), src/syntax.c (6426), src/store.c
(595), src/dnf.c (378), src/big.c (147), core.h (486), drivers.c (211), sha256.c (45); the
classification of every part is `core-forced-by-paper`.

A term is a net of cells joined by wires (core.h, lines 1 to 10). A cell has a kind and a few ports;
one port is principal. Values provide on their principal port, operations consume there. An active
pair is an operation wired to a value at both principal ports; a rule replaces the two cells by a
small net on their other wires (net.c, "the rules", lines 2218 onward in the version read). Running
is performing active pairs until none remains. Sharing is a cell: a value read twice is wired through
a duplicator (`O_DUP`), which copies the value's top cell when it arrives and wires the parts through
new duplicators, one level at a time; a value read by nobody meets an eraser (`O_ERA`) and is freed
level by level. There is no tree anywhere: what Agda would copy, the net duplicates lazily; what
Agda's garbage collector would find, the net erases as it goes.

Call by need. net.c, "Call by need", lines 4150 onward: every operand is compiled unrun, built as a
deferred cell (`V_DEFER`: the operation's kind, payload and inputs) and run by its reader, held once by
a duplicator, erased unrun if nobody reads it. The strict exceptions are the inputs an operation
always reads (`syn_reads`).

Substitution is held, not pushed. net.c, lines 1185 to 1208 in the version read, `rule_subst`: a
substitution of dimensions meeting a value with parts puts the value in a box and makes a reference
carrying the substitution; the substitution is applied one level when the reference is read
(`ref_read`), and composed with any substitution already pending (`subst_compose`, 2.9% of the
reading's samples). A value never read under the substitution is never substituted.

Faces and intervals are canonical forms. src/dnf.c: an interval expression is a join of meets of
literals (a dimension or its negation) kept as an antichain, a face formula a join of meets of
equations `i = 0`, `i = 1`; meet, join, negation and substitution produce canonical forms, and 0, 1
and equality are read off; payloads are immutable and reference-counted, single literals immortal.
A face is decided once by comparison, not decomposed per use.

Code is compiled once into records. lang.c, "compiling" (1773 onward) and "interning compiled code"
(1920): a definition's body is a `Syn` tree with binders as distances and names as definition
numbers; a λ or a thunk carries a pointer to its record, and equal records are one pointer. Building
a net from a record (`build_m`) follows pointers; there is no lookup by name during a run.

The checker is a program on the net (lib/check.cub); it is not involved in the reading measured here
except that the definitions it accepted are the ones built.

The free-dimension cut, net.c lines 941 to 1184 (A8 and A8b, from PLAN.md revision 7.9, §18.16
"A8 — Proposal: formal transported compositions"): once a value is built, walk it for the dimensions
it mentions and keep the set on its box, so a pending substitution of other dimensions is dropped
(A8); for an operation still pending, bound the set by its inputs' (A8b). This is the one mechanism
measured in §5.1; A8b was turned off by default at 8dcef6d36 on Avik's word and its code removed at
ecf0dc6c9 (src/net.c git object c966f478c5dae452b4af3db7ce4e1cbc4d8a966a), the outputs of the
rebuilt machine equal to the switched-off ones, the reading 0.069 s median over thirty rounds after
the removal.

## 5. The profiles

### 5.1 One mechanism of ours, switched

The reading's own time with each part of the cut off, thirty interleaved rounds by the step-counter
clock (a8b.log):

| machine | median | mean ± se | steps |
|---|---|---|---|
| as it was (A8 and A8b on) | 0.167 s | 0.174 ± 0.004 | 358,691 |
| A8 off, A8b on | 0.088 s | 0.095 ± 0.003 | 405,568 |
| A8 on, A8b off | 0.070 s | 0.077 ± 0.002 | 395,608 |
| both off | 0.088 s | 0.092 ± 0.002 | 405,568 |

Fewer steps, more time: the bound on pending operations walked the pending net (fd_pending,
fd_port, fd_cell, box_fd) at a cost above the 13% of steps it saved. Over whole runs it changed
nothing (Brunerie 2.65 s against 2.67 s, the library 45.3 s against 45.0 s, twelve pairs each; a8-whole.log); values and verdicts
identical either way (262 and 2810 passed, 0 failed), the step counts printed not. The memo table
(one value per key, so that two producers of one term shared one value) was measured once, at
e14aaba1 on 2026-11-17, the last machine that had it: switched off, every value and verdict the
same, 13% more steps on the reading, no difference in whole-run time over twelve pairs
(memo-timing.log). Another session then removed the memo and the keys from the machine on
2026-11-18 (commit 0bace3c2e, Avik: "two processes unaware of each other computing the same thing
share nothing"), so the hundred-pair "memo on against off" series of 2026-11-20 (more-runs.log)
measured one machine against itself and says nothing; I did not re-read the current net before
running it, and the node said the memo was present until 2026-11-21. So of the machine's mechanisms
that could be switched, one contributed nothing to this computation's speed (the memo, since
removed) and one less than nothing (the bound, since removed). Whatever makes the reading fast is in
what cannot be switched off: the net itself, the deferred operands, the held substitutions, the
canonical faces, the compiled records.

The third switch the machine has is its worker count (`CUBICAL_WORKERS`; every measurement in this
document is on one worker). Four workers against one, whole runs, alternating (workers-timing.log,
sha256 993da800…): Brunerie 8.57 s against 2.82 s (12 pairs; 17.0 s of processor time against 2.7),
the library 527 s against 44.5 s (3 pairs against 4; 781 s against 44); outputs identical but for
counts. The parallel machine is three to twelve times slower than the sequential one and burns six to
eighteen times the processor time: whatever the Chase–Lev deques and the shard locks were written for,
on this hardware and these workloads they are a cost, and the comparison with Agda above is of one
thread against one thread.

