### 5.3 Where Agda's computation spends its time

Agda has no window of its own that perf can see, so the computation's profile is a difference: perf
at 4 kHz on the module that loads `brunerie'` without computing it, and on a module that computes it
twenty times (`t01 … t20 : brunerie' ≡ -2`, each `refl`; Agda checks each separately, and its
profiler confirms the twenty cost 4.73 s, 0.236 s each, the same as one). Three recordings of each,
pooled: 30,558 samples loading, 91,126 computing twenty times; the difference, 64,954 samples, is the
twenty computations with the load subtracted (pa-diff.sym). By module (GHC's symbol names decoded):

| share | part | what it is |
|---|---|---|
| 34% | GHC's garbage collector | evacuate 23.9% of the whole run, scavenge_block 3.7%, compaction, GC bookkeeping |
| 18% | kernel, libc and unresolved addresses | page faults and clearing (the heap growing), spin locks, addresses perf could not name |
| 12% | GHC's runtime and libraries | thunk update frames (stg_upd_frame), partial applications (stg_PAP_apply, stg_ap_*), Data.Map and IntMap |
| 10.5% | the type-checking monad | `HasBuiltins ReduceM` lookups 4.4%, `BuiltinAccess` 2.1%, signature lookup (`lookupCont`) 1.8%, `defaultGetConstInfo` 0.4% |
| 7.5% | Substitute: `mkPiSort` | computing the sort of a Π type while substituting (`mkPiSort_subE1` 4.6%, `mkPiSort31` 2.8%) |
| 4.3% | Reduce.Fast | the abstract machine proper (`goETS` 1.0%, `fastReduce'` 0.8%, `compactDef` 0.7%) |
| 3.2% | Reduce | the slow reducer and `Normalise` instances |
| 3.1% | Substitute (other) | `defApp` 1.1%, `lookupS'` 0.6%, `applyE` |
| 1.7% | Primitive.Cubical | `intervalView'` 0.5% and the rest |
| 1.8% | Syntax | term constructors and traversals |
| 2% | everything else | |

Two cautions. The 18% "kernel and unresolved" is partly page faults from a heap that grows by
464 MB per computation (which is the collector's cost by another name) and partly addresses without
symbols; it is not attributed. And a difference of two sampled runs carries the load's noise; the
groups above 3% are stable across the three recordings, the ones under 2% are not to be read closely.

### 5.3a Agda's own cost centres: what the computation does, counted

A second Agda 2.9.0 was built with GHC profiling (every package, cost centres on top-level functions;
binary sha256 47eb35558ae6d4613204946ab26a48fd73edaa815de8908bd30915096fd71793, GHC 9.4.7, the
same source tarball) and run with `+RTS -p` on the twenty-fold computing module and on the loading
module (profiles agda-TimeCompute20Exp.prof, sha256 31eae6fe…, and agda-TimeLoadExp2.prof, a72a6ba2…; the
profiled binary's first run, agda-TimeLoadExp.prof 8342b973…, re-checked the whole library because
its interfaces are its own, and is the library's profile, not the load's). A profiled build is slower
and its time shares are distorted by the cost centres themselves, so the times are not compared with
§5.3; what a profile counts exactly is entries: how many times each function was called. The
computing run's count less the loading run's, divided by twenty, per computation of `brunerie'`:

| what Agda did, per computation | times |
|---|---|
| entered its abstract machine (`fastReduce'`) | 270,100 |
| computed a compact definition for it (`compactDef`); its memo table's entries (`memoQName`) | 493,300; 1,048,200 |
| fell back to the slow reducer (`slowReduceTerm`) | 94,200 |
| decoded a closure to a term (`decodeClosure`) | 934,200 |
| performed a Kan operation (`primTransHComp`: transport or composition) | 24,500 |
| viewed an interval expression (`intervalView'`) | 564,900 |
| decomposed a face formula (`decomposeInterval'`) | 108,900 |
| applied a substitution to a term (`applySubst`) | 5,420,900 |
| traversed an argument structure (`traverse` of Agda.Syntax.Common) | 1,049,400 |
| reduced a term (`reduceB'`) | 2,927,800 |
| asked the monad for a builtin (`getBuiltinThing`) | 16,907,800 |
| compared two terms (`compareTerm'`) | 84,700 |
| allocated (the runtime's count, unprofiled) | 464 MB |

Against the machine's 395,608 interaction steps for the same computation. The loading run's own
counts, subtracted above, are of checking the test's type: 1,232,066 machine entries, 6,434,314
builtin lookups, 257,551 substitutions, no Kan operation.

What the counts settle. The abstract machine is not entered once per computation and left running: it
is entered 270,100 times, each entry computing its compact definitions afresh (its memoisation of
`getConstInfo` lives inside one entry, Fast.hs lines 6 to 12 and 470 to 489: 493,300 compact
definitions per computation), and it falls back to the slow reducer 94,200 times, decoding 934,200
closures to terms on the way: 38 decodes per Kan operation. That is conjecture (b) of §6 made a count:
the sharing the machine builds is written out as trees nearly a million times per computation, at the
primitives this computation is made of. And 16.9 million builtin lookups and 5.4 million
substitutions against 395,608 interactions says the same of (e) and (c): every step of Agda's
reduction asks its state for the names of the interval's constants, where a cell of the net has its
kind, and substitutes through terms where the net holds the substitution on a reference.

### 5.4 Side by side

| | Agda 2.9.0 | the machine |
|---|---|---|
| the computation | 0.236 s | 0.074 s |
| memory management | garbage collection 34% + heap growth in the kernel's share: about 40% | the C allocator 20% (plus the erasures inside the 47%) |
| the evaluator's own stepping | abstract machine 4% + slow reducer 3% + cubical primitives 2%: about 9% | interactions 47% |
| substitution | 7.5% sorts of Π + 3.1% other: about 11% | held substitutions and faces 13% |
| finding definitions and builtins | 10.5% (monad lookups) | 0 (pointers in records; build 5%) |
| the runtime's own machinery | 12% (thunks, applications, maps) | 0 |

Roughly a tenth of Agda's time is its evaluator evaluating; roughly half of ours is. The rest of
Agda's is memory it allocates and collects, lookups it repeats, and sorts it recomputes.

## 6. Why the machine is faster: what is known, what is conjecture

Known, from the measurements above:

1. The lead is 3.2 times against current Agda on this computation (0.074 s against 0.236 s), 4.5
   against the version the library declares, and it was 1.5 until a mechanism of ours that cost time
   was switched off. No other mechanism of ours that can be switched contributes to the lead.
2. Agda's computation allocates about 464 MB and spends about 38% of its time collecting it (two
   independent measures agree: the runtime's own statistics and the sampled profile). The machine's
   allocator takes 20% of the reading, and its cells are recycled through per-thread free lists and
   slabs (net.c, "cells and variables", lines 17 to 193).
3. About 10% of Agda's computation is looking things up in its monad's state (builtins, the
   signature) and 7.5% is recomputing the sorts of Π types under substitution; neither has a
   counterpart in the machine, where a compiled record points at what it uses and a type's sort is
   never recomputed by a run.
4. The parts of Agda that are the evaluator (the abstract machine, the slow reducer, the cubical
   primitives) are about 9% of its computation; the machine's interactions are 47% of its reading.

Conjecture, honestly marked, in the order of how much I would bet on each:

- (a) Memory is the first difference. A tree evaluator builds a new tree for every substitution
  and every reduct and leaves the old to the collector; 464 MB for one integer is the size of that.
  A net rewrites in place: a rule frees its two cells and allocates a handful, and a value nobody reads
  is erased level by level instead of surviving to a collection. If Agda's 40% of memory management
  and most of its runtime overhead went to zero, its 0.236 s would be near 0.12 s, still not 0.074 s:
  memory is about half the gap, on these numbers, not all of it.
- (b) Sharing lost at the cubical primitives. Agda's fast machine shares by thunks, but a Kan
  operation or a Glue is not among what it computes; it decodes the closure to a term and hands it to
  the slow reducer (Fast.hs 1466 to 1478), which works on trees: at exactly the operations this
  computation is made of (transport along `ua`, composition in the truncations, unglue), the sharing
  the machine had is written out as copies. The net has one evaluator for everything, and a Kan
  operation is a cell like any other, its arguments shared through duplicators like any other. The
  3.2% of slow reducer against 4.3% of fast machine in the profile is consistent with this, not proof
  of it; proof would be a count of decoded closure sizes, which Agda does not print.
- (c) Substitution of dimensions. Every face case of a composition substitutes a dimension by 0 or 1
  through the term in Agda (applySubstTerm rebuilds every layer, recomputing Π sorts on the way:
  the 7.5%). The machine holds the substitution on a reference and applies it one level as the value is
  read; a part never read is never substituted (rule_subst, ref_read; 13% of the reading goes to
  substitutions and faces, including their composition). On a computation whose terms are large
  cubes read at a few faces, this is the difference between work proportional to the term and work
  proportional to what is read.
- (d) Faces decided once. Agda decomposes a face formula into assignments by a cartesian product of
  its syntax each time a system is matched (decomposeInterval', Base.hs 354 to 391); the machine's
  faces are canonical normal forms compared in place (dnf.c). The profile puts Agda's cubical
  primitives at 1.7% and our face operations inside 13%, so this is not where the gap is, on these
  numbers; it is listed because it is a real difference of method.
- (e) Lookups. 10% of Agda's computation is lookups the machine does not do. This is known, not
  conjecture; what is conjecture is that it is a consequence of evaluating inside a type-checking
  monad rather than from compiled code, and so not removable by tuning.

What would turn conjecture into measurement: on our side, switches for the deferred operands, the
held substitutions and the record interning (each off in turn, the reading timed, as the cut was);
on Agda's side, a build with GHC profiling (`-prof`), which attributes time to cost centres across
the monad and the collector, and a count of the terms the fast machine decodes at each fallback; and
for both, a measure of work common to the two (the number of Kan operations performed, the number of
face cases taken) that neither prints today.

## 7. What is not claimed

Not that the machine is faster than every cubical implementation: no other has run this computation
(the paper's conclusion: "It would be interesting to run this in other cubical systems, like
cubicaltt, redtt, cooltt"; Tom Jack's cubicaltt result is a different Brunerie number). Not that it
is faster on β₃: unported. Not that the lead holds on other computations: lib/library.cub's 2810
checks are a different workload (the front end, compiling and interning, is 12% there and the
interactions 30%, evaluator-speed). Not that the Agda build here is the fastest possible Agda: GHC
9.4.7 with Hackage's flags; a newer GHC or a different runtime configuration (a larger nursery would
cut the collector's share) was not tried, and the 2.9.0 against 2.8.0.2 difference shows the system
moving. And not that the cause is understood: §6 is measured as far as it goes and conjecture past
that, and says which is which.
